# course|lesson|slot|title|passage|prompt|answer|wrong1|wrong2|wrong3|evidence|explanation
suneung-2000|1|1|Labels for Everyone|A volunteer at a small cultural museum noticed that visitors often skipped the longest labels. She asked the audience which information they actually wanted. Their answers helped her develop shorter labels beside the exhibits. The new labels did not remove difficult ideas; they explained one idea at a time. Visitors began asking more questions instead of walking past.|새 안내문의 특징은 무엇인가요?|어려운 내용을 한 번에 한 가지씩 설명했다.|전시품의 설명을 모두 없앴다.|방문객이 질문하지 못하게 했다.|전문가만 읽을 수 있는 표현을 늘렸다.|The new labels did not remove difficult ideas; they explained one idea at a time.|어려운 생각을 빼지 않고 하나씩 설명했다고 명시되어 있습니다.
suneung-2000|1|2|Choosing the Material|Our science team had enough money for only a small amount of material. One member wanted the brightest package, but another suggested research before shopping. We tested three samples with the same weight. The least expensive sample held the most weight. That result changed our attitude: appearance could attract attention, but it did not prove that a material was strong.|팀의 구매 기준은 어떻게 바뀌었나요?|겉모습보다 시험에서 확인한 성능을 보게 됐다.|가장 눈에 띄는 포장을 무조건 골랐다.|가격이 높은 재료만 시험했다.|재료의 강도는 비교할 수 없다고 결론냈다.|The least expensive sample held the most weight.|가장 싼 시료가 가장 큰 무게를 견뎌 외관만으로 강도를 판단하지 않게 됐습니다.
suneung-2000|2|1|The Short Ride|Our local bus company wanted more people to ride its weekend bus. Posters showed comfortable seats, yet few passengers came. Drivers asked residents about the problem. The bus left before the market opened and returned before shoppers finished. The company changed the schedule without buying new buses. More people used the service because it now matched their plans.|이용객이 늘어난 직접적인 이유는 무엇인가요?|운행 시간이 주민들의 일정에 맞게 바뀌었다.|모든 버스를 새 버스로 바꿨다.|주말 시장을 폐쇄했다.|좌석 사진을 더 크게 인쇄했다.|More people used the service because it now matched their plans.|시간표를 바꿔 주민들의 계획과 맞아졌기 때문에 이용이 늘었습니다.
suneung-2000|2|2|Keeping the Hall Comfortable|During a school event, every available heater was switched on. The room soon became too warm, and the extra expense worried the organizer. A student measured the temperature in several places instead of guessing from one corner. Two heaters were enough to provide comfort across the room. The remaining heaters were turned off, reducing the energy the event would consume.|학생은 난방 상태를 어떻게 확인했나요?|방의 여러 곳에서 온도를 측정했다.|한쪽 구석의 느낌만 믿었다.|사용 가능한 난방기를 모두 버렸다.|행사를 열지 않는 것으로 결정했다.|A student measured the temperature in several places instead of guessing from one corner.|한 지점의 느낌이 아니라 여러 장소의 측정값을 이용했습니다.
suneung-2000|3|1|Before the Performance|Before the performance, Hana worried that her nervous emotion would ruin the evening. Her teacher did not demand that she feel perfectly calm. Instead, they practiced where to look after the first line and when to take a breath. These small actions gave Hana something useful to focus on. She still felt nervous on stage, but she completed her part.|연습이 하나에게 준 도움은 무엇인가요?|긴장한 상태에서도 집중할 구체적인 행동을 마련했다.|긴장을 완전히 없애야만 무대에 설 수 있게 했다.|공연 전에 모든 대사를 바꾸게 했다.|다른 사람의 역할까지 맡게 했다.|These small actions gave Hana something useful to focus on.|시선과 호흡 같은 작은 행동이 긴장 속에서도 집중할 대상을 주었습니다.
suneung-2000|3|2|A Fair Chemistry Test|Two groups in chemistry class wanted to discover which cloth absorbed more water. One group used a large piece and the other used a small piece. Their first results could not settle the issue because cloth size could affect the outcome. They repeated the test with equal pieces. Only then did they compare how much water each material could hold.|첫 시험의 결과를 바로 비교할 수 없었던 이유는?|천 조각의 크기가 달랐다.|두 집단이 같은 양의 물을 사용했다.|두 천의 색이 완전히 같았다.|흡수량을 기록한 사람이 너무 많았다.|Their first results could not settle the issue because cloth size could affect the outcome.|천의 크기 자체가 결과에 영향을 줄 수 있어 크기를 맞춰 다시 시험했습니다.
suneung-2000|4|1|The Missing Package|A package began to disappear behind other boxes in the school office. Its owner called several times, but nobody could describe it in enough detail. A clerk introduced a simple approach: every delivery received a numbered sticker and a place on a shelf. The system was not complex. It worked because everyone could contact the office using the same number.|새 방식이 효과적이었던 이유는 무엇인가요?|모두 같은 배송 번호로 물건을 확인할 수 있었다.|배송 상자를 받는 즉시 열어 보았다.|상자를 눈에 띄지 않는 곳으로 옮겼다.|물건의 색깔만 기억하도록 했다.|It worked because everyone could contact the office using the same number.|번호라는 공통 기준을 사용해 배송 물건을 정확히 확인할 수 있었습니다.
suneung-2000|4|2|A Different Career Picture|Min read a novel about a person whose career changed several times. At first, he thought a secure profession meant doing exactly the same work forever. The character's experience offered another view. Her useful skills remained, even when her workplace changed. Min became aware that learning how to solve a problem could matter more than memorizing one job title.|민이 새롭게 생각하게 된 것은 무엇인가요?|직업 이름보다 문제 해결 능력이 오래 쓰일 수 있다.|한 직장에서만 일해야 능력을 유지할 수 있다.|소설 속 경험은 어떤 생각도 바꿀 수 없다.|직업을 바꾸면 이전 능력은 모두 사라진다.|Her useful skills remained, even when her workplace changed.|일터가 바뀌어도 유용한 능력은 남았다는 경험이 민의 관점을 바꿨습니다.
suneung-2000|5|1|Borrow Before Buying|The biology club wanted new equipment for an experiment. An expert visited the club and asked students to describe what they already owned. They found several usable tools in a cupboard and borrowed the missing item from another class. They did not need to replace everything. Their desire for a fresh start had almost hidden the value of existing resources.|동아리가 새 물건을 모두 살 필요가 없었던 이유는?|쓸 수 있는 도구를 찾고 부족한 것은 빌렸기 때문이다.|실험을 영구히 취소했기 때문이다.|전문가가 모든 장비를 버리라고 했기 때문이다.|필요한 도구가 하나도 없었기 때문이다.|They found several usable tools in a cupboard and borrowed the missing item from another class.|기존 물품을 확인하고 대여해 필요한 도구를 마련했습니다.
suneung-2000|5|2|An Honest Account|Our newspaper planned to publish an account of the recycling project. The first draft said that waste had fallen every week, but the records showed one increase. We could not describe the change as constant. We included the unusual week and explained that a festival had produced extra rubbish. The less perfect story gave readers a clearer picture of the project.|신문이 한 주의 증가를 포함한 이유는?|기록에 맞는 정확한 경과를 보여 주기 위해서였다.|재활용 사업이 전혀 효과 없다고 주장하려고 했다.|축제 이야기를 기사에서 모두 빼기 위해서였다.|감소 기록을 숨기려고 했다.|The less perfect story gave readers a clearer picture of the project.|예외가 있던 주까지 설명해야 독자가 사업의 실제 변화를 이해할 수 있었습니다.
suneung-2000|6|1|A Quiet Exchange|A lonely student rarely spoke during lunch. His classmates assumed that he preferred being alone, until a partner asked him about music. He liked to compose short songs but felt unsure about sharing them. They arranged a weekly exchange of recordings. The steady routine gave him a reason to join the group without requiring him to speak loudly in a crowded room.|정기적인 녹음 교환이 학생에게 준 것은?|편안한 방식으로 모임에 참여할 기회였다.|큰 소리로 말해야 하는 시험이었다.|작곡을 그만둘 이유였다.|혼자 식사하도록 정한 규칙이었다.|The steady routine gave him a reason to join the group without requiring him to speak loudly in a crowded room.|시끄러운 곳에서 크게 말하지 않아도 음악을 통해 참여할 수 있게 됐습니다.
suneung-2000|6|2|The Changed Habitat|After an extreme storm, a class examined a small pond habitat. Students expected every species to react in the same way. Instead, some insects gathered near fallen branches while others moved into open water. The teacher asked them to reflect on each change before naming a cause. A single factor might influence different living things differently, depending on how they use the pond.|교사가 학생들에게 요구한 태도는 무엇인가요?|변화를 각각 살펴본 뒤 원인을 판단하는 것이다.|모든 생물이 똑같이 반응했다고 기록하는 것이다.|폭풍 이전의 관찰은 모두 버리는 것이다.|한 종만 보고 연못 전체를 판단하는 것이다.|The teacher asked them to reflect on each change before naming a cause.|원인을 단정하기 전에 각 생물의 변화를 따로 검토하도록 했습니다.
suneung-2000|7|1|An Accessible Night|The astronomy club was ready to announce a night of star watching. One member noticed that the only telescope stood above several steps. A disabled visitor might have no access to it. The club placed another telescope on the level path and tested the route before opening. The change did not alter the stars, but it changed who could enjoy looking at them.|동아리가 망원경을 추가로 놓은 이유는?|계단을 이용하기 어려운 사람도 관찰하게 하려는 것이었다.|별의 위치를 바꾸려는 것이었다.|방문객의 질문을 줄이려는 것이었다.|천문 행사를 낮에만 열려는 것이었다.|The club placed another telescope on the level path and tested the route before opening.|평평한 길에 망원경을 마련해 관찰 기회의 접근성을 높였습니다.
suneung-2000|7|2|Concentrating in the Library|Some students found it hard to concentrate near the library entrance. The librarian first considered a firm rule against all conversation. Before deciding, she asked for a response from regular visitors. They suggested separate quiet and discussion areas. Noise did not disappear completely, but it no longer filled every corner. The library could allow collaboration without removing space for individual study.|도서관이 채택한 해결책은 무엇인가요?|조용한 구역과 토론 구역을 나눴다.|모든 대화를 영구히 금지했다.|개인 학습 공간을 없앴다.|방문객의 의견을 받지 않았다.|They suggested separate quiet and discussion areas.|공간을 나누어 협력과 개인 공부가 함께 이루어지도록 했습니다.
suneung-2000|8|1|The Bridge Model|A team had to construct a model bridge using a limited quantity of paper. They did not begin by adding more layers everywhere. They tested how pressure moved through different shapes, then chose where extra support was needed. Their final bridge used less material than an earlier model but held more weight. Careful design had mattered more than simply making the whole bridge thicker.|마지막 모형이 더 적은 재료로 버틴 이유는?|필요한 곳에 지지 구조를 배치했기 때문이다.|아무 부분도 시험하지 않았기 때문이다.|모든 곳을 똑같이 두껍게 만들었기 때문이다.|다리 위에 무게를 올리지 않았기 때문이다.|They tested how pressure moved through different shapes, then chose where extra support was needed.|압력이 전달되는 방식을 시험해 보강할 위치를 정했습니다.
suneung-2000|8|2|Plans for the Launch|A club planned to launch a small paper boat during a festival. Heavy rain created a flood threat near the stream, frustrating the organizers. They moved the activity to a shallow indoor pool instead of asking visitors to approach the fast water. The boats traveled a shorter distance, but everyone could see their movement safely. Changing the location preserved the main activity.|장소를 바꾼 뒤에도 유지된 것은 무엇인가요?|배의 움직임을 관찰하는 핵심 활동이었다.|빠른 냇물 가까이 가는 계획이었다.|원래와 같은 이동 거리였다.|홍수 위험이 있는 장소의 사용이었다.|Changing the location preserved the main activity.|실내로 옮겨 안전을 확보하면서 배를 띄우고 관찰하는 활동은 유지했습니다.
suneung-2000|9|1|One Clear Instruction|An electronic display in the community center confused visitors. Its instruction showed six actions at once, although most users needed only the first two. A resident suggested revealing the next action after the previous one was complete. During a second test, more people finished without help. The device had gained no new feature; its existing features had become easier to understand.|두 번째 시험에서 이용이 쉬워진 이유는?|해야 할 행동이 순서대로 표시됐기 때문이다.|새 기능이 여섯 개 추가됐기 때문이다.|첫 두 단계가 삭제됐기 때문이다.|방문객에게 사용을 금지했기 때문이다.|A resident suggested revealing the next action after the previous one was complete.|이전 행동을 끝내면 다음 안내를 보여 주어 한꺼번에 처리할 정보가 줄었습니다.
suneung-2000|9|2|What to Donate|Our class wanted to donate supplies to a neighborhood workshop. We planned to collect a wide variety of items, but the workers asked us to check their stock first. They already had many pencils and almost no protective gloves. We changed the collection list. Asking about a real need gave our effort an advantage that a larger, less focused collection would not have had.|학생들이 모금 물품 목록을 바꾼 이유는?|작업장의 실제 부족 물품을 확인했기 때문이다.|다양한 물품은 언제나 쓸모없기 때문이다.|작업장에 연필이 전혀 없었기 때문이다.|보호 장갑을 사용하지 않기로 했기 때문이다.|They already had many pencils and almost no protective gloves.|연필은 충분했지만 장갑이 부족하다는 정보를 바탕으로 목록을 조정했습니다.
suneung-2000|10|1|Before the Tide Returned|A stranger at the harbor asked our group to leave the rocks. One student felt embarrassed and wanted to argue, but our guide asked for a reason. The rising tide would soon cover the narrow path back to shore. We moved immediately and later thanked the stranger. An unwelcome remark had contained information that could prevent a dangerous mistake.|학생들이 바위에서 나온 결정적 이유는?|돌아갈 길이 밀물에 잠길 예정이었기 때문이다.|낯선 사람이 사진을 찍고 싶어 했기 때문이다.|안내자가 항구를 처음 방문했기 때문이다.|바위가 이미 모두 무너졌기 때문이다.|The rising tide would soon cover the narrow path back to shore.|밀물이 귀환 경로를 덮을 위험이 있다는 설명을 듣고 이동했습니다.
suneung-2000|10|2|A Reasonable Request|The art room had a rule that students could not interrupt a demonstration. One afternoon, a student noticed water reaching an electrical cable and called out. The teacher stopped the lesson and asked someone to obtain help. Later, they revised the rule to include urgent safety concerns. An efficient classroom needed a reasonable exception, not silence in every possible situation.|수업 규칙은 어떻게 바뀌었나요?|긴급한 안전 문제는 즉시 알릴 수 있게 됐다.|어떤 상황에서도 말을 못 하게 됐다.|시범 수업을 모두 없앴다.|학생이 위험을 혼자 해결하게 했다.|Later, they revised the rule to include urgent safety concerns.|일반적인 방해는 줄이되 긴급한 안전 우려에는 예외를 두었습니다.
suneung-2000|11|1|A Clue at the Edge|During a practice treasure hunt, our group found a clue near the edge of a garden. The note said to look beside the oldest tree, not to climb it. One player wanted to search the high branches anyway. We decided to consider the specific instruction first. The next envelope lay safely among the roots, and nobody needed to take an unnecessary risk.|그룹이 위험을 감수할 필요가 없었던 이유는?|단서가 나무 옆을 가리켰고 봉투가 뿌리 사이에 있었기 때문이다.|봉투가 높은 나뭇가지 끝에 있었기 때문이다.|단서가 나무에 오르라고 명령했기 때문이다.|게임에서 모든 나무가 제거됐기 때문이다.|The next envelope lay safely among the roots, and nobody needed to take an unnecessary risk.|지시대로 나무 옆을 확인하자 뿌리 사이에서 봉투를 찾았습니다.
suneung-2000|11|2|Testing the Theory|An independent team studied why attendance at a reading club had fallen. Their first theory blamed the choice of books. Interviews offered another clue: several members could no longer reach the meeting after their bus schedule changed. The team moved the meeting later and checked attendance again. The improved result suggested that practical access, rather than dislike of reading, had been the main problem.|새 조사 결과가 보여 준 주된 문제는?|책에 대한 흥미보다 모임에 올 수 있는 시간이었다.|모든 회원이 독서를 싫어하게 된 것이었다.|책을 빌릴 자원이 전혀 없던 것이었다.|독립적인 조사 자체가 불가능했던 것이었다.|several members could no longer reach the meeting after their bus schedule changed|버스 시간표가 바뀌어 참석하지 못한다는 단서에 따라 모임 시간을 조정했습니다.
suneung-2000|12|1|A Route Worth Observing|Travelers usually chose the shortest route through our town. A local guide proposed a slower walk past the grain store and the old bridge. She gave a brief explanation at each stop, allowing visitors to observe changes that were easy to miss from a car. The walk took longer, but its value came from noticing places rather than merely reaching the end.|이 산책의 가치는 무엇에 있었나요?|이동 중 지나치기 쉬운 장소를 관찰하는 데 있었다.|최단 시간에 목적지에 도착하는 데 있었다.|어떤 설명도 듣지 않는 데 있었다.|차 안에서만 마을을 보는 데 있었다.|The walk took longer, but its value came from noticing places rather than merely reaching the end.|빠른 도착보다 이동 과정에서 장소를 자세히 보는 것이 중요했습니다.
suneung-2000|12|2|A Meaningful Purchase|After receiving her first salary, Jiwoo wanted to purchase an expensive notebook for her poetry. Before buying it, she visited a free workshop at the library. The instructor helped her acquire a habit of recording small observations each day. A plain notebook was enough for that work. She still liked beautiful covers, but she now understood that the habit made the writing meaningful.|지우가 알게 된 것은 무엇인가요?|의미 있는 글에는 비싼 표지보다 관찰을 기록하는 습관이 중요하다.|시를 쓰려면 고가의 공책이 반드시 필요하다.|무료 수업에서는 어떤 기술도 배울 수 없다.|일상의 작은 관찰은 시에 쓸 수 없다.|she now understood that the habit made the writing meaningful|매일 관찰을 기록하는 습관이 글을 의미 있게 한다는 점을 배웠습니다.
suneung-2000|13|1|The Rural Display|A rural museum wanted a display about farming tools. Its first design surrounded each object with a long technical comment. Visitors looked exhausted before reaching the second case. The museum replaced most of the text with a short story about one typical day's work. People began connecting the tools with real actions, instead of treating them as a series of unrelated objects.|전시를 바꾸자 도구가 어떻게 이해되기 시작했나요?|실제 하루의 작업과 연결된 물건으로 이해됐다.|서로 관계없는 번호로만 이해됐다.|사용 방법을 알 수 없는 장식품으로만 이해됐다.|전문가만 볼 수 있는 비밀 자료로 이해됐다.|People began connecting the tools with real actions, instead of treating them as a series of unrelated objects.|하루의 작업 이야기가 각 도구의 쓰임을 연결해 주었습니다.
suneung-2000|13|2|The Mechanic's Pause|Our bicycle mechanic stopped an operation that seemed almost complete. A small part did not fit as expected. Rather than force it into place, she chose to seek the correct instructions and compare the model numbers. The delay changed our impatient mood. We realized that a careful pause could protect the whole bicycle from damage that a quick finish might cause.|정비사가 작업을 잠시 멈춘 이유는?|부품이 맞지 않아 정확한 지침을 확인하려고 했다.|자전거를 고칠 능력이 없다고 선언하려고 했다.|고객에게 더 비싼 자전거만 팔려고 했다.|이미 모든 작업이 완벽히 끝났기 때문이다.|Rather than force it into place, she chose to seek the correct instructions and compare the model numbers.|맞지 않는 부품을 억지로 끼우지 않고 지침과 모델 번호부터 확인했습니다.
suneung-2000|14|1|A Survey with a Gap|A committee used an online survey to decide where a new facility should be built. The results looked accurate because hundreds of answers arrived. Then a member noticed a gap: residents without reliable internet had rarely responded. The committee added paper forms at the market. The new answers did not cancel the earlier ones; they made the overall picture more complete.|종이 설문을 추가한 목적은 무엇인가요?|온라인 조사에서 빠지기 쉬운 주민 의견을 받기 위해서였다.|먼저 받은 답을 모두 없애기 위해서였다.|인터넷 이용자만 참여시키기 위해서였다.|응답 수를 일부러 줄이기 위해서였다.|residents without reliable internet had rarely responded|인터넷 이용이 어려운 주민들이 거의 응답하지 않아 다른 참여 방식을 마련했습니다.
suneung-2000|14|2|Planning the Journey|For a domestic journey, our group planned to visit six places in one afternoon. A guide asked us to arrange the stops on a map and attach travel times to each route. The total was longer than the afternoon itself. We removed two stops and left time to rest. A shorter list gave us a more realistic journey, not necessarily a poorer one.|두 장소를 일정에서 뺀 이유는?|이동 시간을 합치니 이용 가능한 시간을 넘었기 때문이다.|모든 장소가 문을 닫았기 때문이다.|지도가 어떤 정보도 제공하지 않았기 때문이다.|국내 여행에서는 휴식이 금지되기 때문이다.|The total was longer than the afternoon itself.|실제 이동 시간의 합이 오후 시간보다 길어 일정을 줄였습니다.
suneung-2000|15|1|The Borrowed Device|A classmate let me borrow a recording device for an urban sound project. I promised to return it the next morning, but a file problem threatened to delay my work. Instead of keeping it without permission, I explained the problem and asked about her schedule. She needed it at noon. We agreed on a new return time that respected both projects.|화자가 문제를 해결한 방법은 무엇인가요?|상황을 설명하고 상대 일정에 맞춰 새 반환 시간을 정했다.|허락 없이 기기를 계속 보관했다.|녹음 자료를 모두 버리고 연락을 끊었다.|반환 약속이 없었다고 주장했다.|We agreed on a new return time that respected both projects.|상대의 필요 시간을 확인해 두 사람의 계획을 고려한 합의를 했습니다.
suneung-2000|15|2|What the Witness Knew|A witness saw a blue bicycle beside the gate shortly before a bag disappeared. People asked her to reveal who had taken it. She could describe the bicycle, but she could not identify its rider or connect it to the missing bag. She refused to suppose more than she had seen. Her careful statement preserved the difference between an observation and a guess.|목격자가 지킨 원칙은 무엇인가요?|직접 본 것과 추측을 구별하는 것이었다.|파란 자전거의 주인이 범인이라고 단정하는 것이었다.|기억나지 않는 내용을 만들어 말하는 것이었다.|관찰한 내용을 모두 숨기는 것이었다.|Her careful statement preserved the difference between an observation and a guess.|본 사실을 넘어서 범인이나 연관성을 추측하지 않았습니다.
suneung-2000|16|1|Reading the Label|In an underground museum, a small label described a row of ancient containers. It listed dates but said nothing about their use. A visitor suggested adding a drawing of someone carrying water. The new picture helped people connect the objects with daily life in an earlier civilization. The objects had not changed, but the explanation gave visitors a clearer way to understand them.|새 그림이 한 역할은 무엇인가요?|유물을 옛사람들의 일상과 연결해 주었다.|유물의 제작 연도를 바꿨다.|모든 글자 안내를 금지했다.|물건의 실제 모양을 숨겼다.|The new picture helped people connect the objects with daily life in an earlier civilization.|물을 나르는 그림이 용기의 생활 속 쓰임을 보여 주었습니다.
suneung-2000|16|2|Preserving a Unique Spot|The town planned to decorate a quiet spot beside the river with bright lights. Residents liked the idea, but an evening walk revealed that many people came there to see the horizon without glare. The town changed its target from maximum brightness to safe, gentle lighting. It could preserve the atmosphere that made the place unique while still helping visitors find the path.|조명 계획의 목표가 어떻게 바뀌었나요?|최대한 밝게 하기보다 안전하면서 은은하게 비추기로 했다.|방문객이 길을 찾지 못하게 하기로 했다.|강변의 모든 길을 없애기로 했다.|수평선을 볼 수 없도록 벽을 세우기로 했다.|The town changed its target from maximum brightness to safe, gentle lighting.|장소 특유의 분위기를 유지하면서 길을 찾을 정도의 조명을 선택했습니다.
suneung-2000|17|1|An Automatic Reply|A regional office installed an automatic message system. Every request received an immediate reply, which made the service seem efficient. Yet several residents were disappointed because the replies did not identify their actual problem. The secretary suggested a simple distinction: routine requests could use the system, while unusual ones went to a person. Fast answers were useful only when they were also relevant.|자동 응답의 한계는 무엇이었나요?|일부 사람의 실제 문제를 알아내지 못했다.|어떤 요청에도 답하지 않았다.|답변을 보내는 데 항상 한 달이 걸렸다.|직원이 직접 답하는 것을 기술적으로 막지 못했다.|the replies did not identify their actual problem|빠른 답변이라도 이용자의 실제 문제에 맞지 않으면 충분하지 않았습니다.
suneung-2000|17|2|A Realistic Model|Our group built an artificial landscape for a geography display. The mountains looked impressive, but the rivers were wider than nearby towns. A classmate asked us to check the scale. We used the same standard for every distance and rebuilt several parts. The result looked less dramatic, yet it showed the region more realistically. A convincing appearance had not guaranteed accurate proportions.|모형을 고친 핵심 기준은 무엇인가요?|모든 거리에 같은 축척을 적용하는 것이었다.|강만 더 크게 만드는 것이었다.|인상적인 모습만 유지하는 것이었다.|마을과 산의 위치를 무작위로 바꾸는 것이었다.|We used the same standard for every distance and rebuilt several parts.|각 부분에 동일한 거리 기준을 적용해 실제 비율에 가까워졌습니다.
suneung-2000|18|1|One Request, Many Voices|Students could vote on a small grant for a school project. One group requested expensive equipment; another asked for reusable supplies that several clubs could share. Before voting, students examined how each application would benefit people beyond its own group. The shared supplies won. The decision did not mean that equipment was useless, but that cooperation offered more value under the limited budget.|공용 물품 제안이 선택된 이유는 무엇인가요?|제한된 예산으로 여러 모임이 함께 혜택을 받을 수 있어서였다.|장비는 어떤 상황에서도 쓸모없기 때문이다.|투표 전에 모든 의견이 금지됐기 때문이다.|한 모임만 사용할 수 있도록 설계됐기 때문이다.|cooperation offered more value under the limited budget|여러 동아리가 공유할 수 있는 이점이 한정된 예산에서 중요하게 평가됐습니다.
suneung-2000|18|2|The Substance Test|In a physics demonstration, a dark substance absorbed light and became warm. One student said that its color must guarantee the same result in every situation. The teacher resisted that conclusion and changed the amount of light reaching the sample. Its temperature changed too. The demonstration showed why the impact of one feature should be tested alongside the conditions in which it operates.|교사가 빛의 양을 바꾼 이유는 무엇인가요?|결과가 색 외의 조건에도 영향을 받는지 보이기 위해서였다.|검은색이면 언제나 같은 온도가 된다고 증명하려고 했다.|시료를 더 이상 관찰하지 않으려고 했다.|실험에서 빛을 전혀 사용하지 않으려고 했다.|Its temperature changed too.|빛의 양을 바꾸자 온도도 달라져 한 가지 특징만으로 모든 결과를 설명할 수 없었습니다.
suneung-2000|19|1|At the Rainforest Entrance|At the entrance to a rainforest exhibit, visitors could choose several paths. The shortest path passed close to a display of small organisms that were difficult to see in a crowd. A guide helped distribute visitors among the routes instead of sending everyone in one direction. Each group had more room to observe, and the location of the smallest exhibit no longer created a crowded bottleneck.|안내원이 방문객을 여러 길로 나눈 이유는?|작은 전시 앞의 혼잡을 줄이고 관찰 공간을 확보하려고 했다.|가장 짧은 길을 영구히 폐쇄하려고 했다.|작은 생물 전시를 보지 못하게 하려고 했다.|모든 관람객을 같은 방향으로 보내려고 했다.|Each group had more room to observe|관람객을 분산하자 각 무리가 전시를 관찰할 공간이 늘었습니다.
suneung-2000|19|2|A Hesitant Partner|My partner hesitated when I suggested an adventure story for our film. I tried to persuade him by describing exciting scenes, but that did not resolve his concern. He worried about filming outdoors during rainy weather. Once I understood the circumstance, we planned an indoor version of the story. Listening to the reason for hesitation helped more than repeating my original offer.|협의가 진행된 전환점은 무엇이었나요?|상대가 꺼리는 실제 이유를 이해한 순간이었다.|흥미로운 장면을 더 큰 소리로 설명한 순간이었다.|상대의 걱정을 무시한 순간이었다.|야외 촬영을 무조건 강요한 순간이었다.|He worried about filming outdoors during rainy weather.|상대의 걱정이 이야기 내용이 아니라 비 오는 날의 야외 촬영임을 알게 됐습니다.
suneung-2000|20|1|The Autograph Queue|After a school symphony concert, a young musician offered to sign programs. The autograph line soon blocked the cabin that stored the instruments. A volunteer suggested moving the line beside the exit and keeping a clear passage for the gear. Nobody lost a chance to meet the musician. A small change in the arrangement protected both the celebration and the work happening behind it.|줄을 옮긴 결과로 알맞은 것은?|만남의 기회를 유지하면서 악기를 옮길 길도 확보했다.|음악가와 만날 기회를 모두 없앴다.|악기를 넣은 공간의 출입을 막았다.|서명을 받은 프로그램을 모두 회수했다.|Nobody lost a chance to meet the musician.|줄의 위치만 조정해 관객의 만남을 유지하며 작업 통로를 비웠습니다.
suneung-2000|20|2|An Alternative Investment|A student company considered an investment in new packaging. The corporate design looked attractive, but it would cost most of the group's money. An alternative used simple boxes and clear instructions about repair. Customers in a small test preferred the second option because it helped them keep the product working. The group learned that an appealing appearance was only one part of a useful purchase.|시험 고객이 두 번째 포장을 선호한 이유는?|제품을 계속 쓸 수 있게 수리 안내가 도움이 됐기 때문이다.|포장 가격이 반드시 가장 높았기 때문이다.|내용물을 볼 수 없게 감쌌기 때문이다.|제품을 한 번만 쓰도록 만들었기 때문이다.|it helped them keep the product working|수리 안내가 제품의 지속적인 사용에 도움을 준다는 이유로 선택했습니다.
suneung-2000|21|1|The Charity Schedule|A charity clinic wanted every infant to receive enough time with its visiting doctor. The first schedule gave each household an identical appointment length. Some families needed an interpreter, however, so their visits took longer. The clinic began asking about language needs before arranging appointments. Treating families fairly required attention to their different situations, rather than assuming that identical time always meant equal help.|진료소가 사전에 언어 지원 필요를 물은 이유는?|가정마다 필요한 진료 시간을 더 적절히 배정하려고 했다.|통역이 필요한 가정은 받지 않으려고 했다.|모든 진료 시간을 무조건 줄이려고 했다.|의사가 가족과 대화하지 않게 하려고 했다.|Some families needed an interpreter, however, so their visits took longer.|통역이 필요한 가정은 시간이 더 들기 때문에 이를 일정에 반영했습니다.
suneung-2000|21|2|The Household Refund|Our household received a refund for a broken kettle. The shop asked whether we wanted an identical replacement or our money back. We chose the money because we already had another kettle we could use. A broad offer of choices was helpful, but the sensible choice still depended on our own situation. A free replacement would not have been useful merely because it was free.|이 가족이 환불을 선택한 이유는?|이미 사용할 수 있는 다른 주전자가 있었기 때문이다.|교환 선택권이 전혀 없었기 때문이다.|새 주전자는 모두 고장 나 있었기 때문이다.|가게가 반드시 두 개를 사라고 했기 때문이다.|We chose the money because we already had another kettle we could use.|필요한 물건이 이미 있어 같은 물건을 하나 더 받는 것보다 환불이 나았습니다.
suneung-2000|22|1|Choosing a Destination|A travel club chose a destination by looking at beautiful photographs. Before they committed to the trip, a member suggested checking the genuine cost of getting there. Transport was cheap, but the nearest affordable rooms were far from the main sites. They compared the sum of travel and lodging costs with another town. The second destination proved less expensive overall, despite its higher train fare.|여행지가 바뀐 이유는 무엇인가요?|교통비와 숙박비를 합한 전체 비용을 비교했기 때문이다.|사진이 모두 가짜였다고 밝혀졌기 때문이다.|두 번째 도시의 기차표가 더 쌌기 때문이다.|숙박 장소는 전혀 필요 없었기 때문이다.|The second destination proved less expensive overall, despite its higher train fare.|기차 요금만이 아니라 숙박까지 포함하면 두 번째 목적지가 더 저렴했습니다.
suneung-2000|22|2|Conserving the Evidence|Our class studied a drawing that showed several stages of a plant's evolution in an imaginary world. The artist had changed every feature at each stage, making relationships hard to follow. We suggested conserving one recognizable pattern while allowing other features to develop. The revised sequence made the concept clearer. Readers could track continuity instead of facing a completely unfamiliar image at every step.|수정된 그림에서 같은 무늬를 남긴 이유는?|단계 사이의 연속성을 알아볼 수 있게 하기 위해서였다.|모든 단계가 완전히 같다는 뜻을 보이기 위해서였다.|상상의 세계를 실제 역사라고 주장하려고 했다.|식물의 특징을 전혀 보여 주지 않으려고 했다.|Readers could track continuity instead of facing a completely unfamiliar image at every step.|일부 알아볼 수 있는 요소를 유지해 변화 전후의 연결을 이해하도록 했습니다.
suneung-2000|23|1|A Clear Invitation|The invitation to a formal school ceremony looked elegant, but several guests missed the starting time. The visual design placed a large sculpture in the center and the departure information in tiny letters below it. We made the time and meeting place more distinct. The new version looked simpler, yet it fulfilled the invitation's main purpose: helping people arrive at the right place and time.|초대장을 수정할 때 강조한 것은 무엇인가요?|행사 시간과 모일 장소였다.|가운데 조각상의 크기였다.|장식의 색깔 수였다.|작은 글씨를 더 작게 만드는 일이었다.|We made the time and meeting place more distinct.|초대장의 실질적인 목적에 필요한 시간과 장소를 더 뚜렷하게 보이도록 했습니다.
suneung-2000|23|2|Following the Leak|An agriculture class noticed that one portion of a garden stayed unusually wet. Students first blamed the current weather, but the rest of the beds were dry. A careful inspection found a leak where two pieces of pipe met. Repairing that single component solved the problem. A general trend could not explain why only one small area differed from everything around it.|날씨가 주된 원인이 아니라고 본 단서는?|다른 화단은 말라 있고 한 부분만 젖어 있었다는 것이다.|모든 화단이 똑같이 젖어 있었다는 것이다.|파이프가 전혀 설치돼 있지 않았다는 것이다.|검사 전부터 새 파이프를 모두 샀다는 것이다.|Students first blamed the current weather, but the rest of the beds were dry.|날씨는 전체에 영향을 주지만 젖은 곳은 국소적이어서 배관을 확인했습니다.
suneung-2000|24|1|A Trap in the Diagram|A guide used a diagram to demonstrate an animal trap from an old story. The picture showed a narrow entrance but no way for the animal to leave. One eager visitor asked whether the diagram described a real device or just the storyteller's idea. The guide checked the source and clarified that it was fictional. A detailed picture was not, by itself, evidence that something had existed.|안내원이 확인한 것은 무엇인가요?|그림은 실제 장치의 기록이 아니라 이야기 속 설정이었다.|그림이 정교하면 반드시 실제 장치였다는 것이다.|관람객이 질문해서는 안 된다는 것이다.|그림 속 동물이 실제 전시장에 있다는 것이다.|The guide checked the source and clarified that it was fictional.|출처 확인을 통해 실제 기록과 허구의 그림을 구분했습니다.
suneung-2000|24|2|Spare Time in a Remote Town|During an annual visit to a remote town, my companion planned every minute. An unexpected road closure forced us to pause near a small workshop. We watched an artist repair a wooden toy and learned about the town's history. On our next visit, we left spare time between appointments. A good plan could guide us without preventing every unplanned discovery.|다음 방문 계획에 여유 시간을 둔 이유는?|예상하지 못한 발견을 할 기회를 남기기 위해서였다.|모든 약속을 지키지 않으려고 했다.|장인의 작업을 방해하려고 했다.|마을의 역사를 더는 듣지 않으려고 했다.|A good plan could guide us without preventing every unplanned discovery.|계획이 일정의 안내 역할을 하면서도 뜻밖의 경험까지 막을 필요는 없다고 느꼈습니다.
suneung-2000|25|1|The Shared Laboratory|A laboratory faced a shortage of a measuring tool. The chief did not immediately request more equipment. She asked the teams to organize their bookings and record when the tool was actually being used. Several teams had reserved whole afternoons for brief tasks. Shorter appointments allowed everyone to finish. The problem had involved access to an existing resource, not simply the number of tools.|도구 부족을 해결한 핵심 조치는?|실제 사용 시간에 맞춰 예약을 짧게 조정한 것이다.|모든 팀의 실험을 취소한 것이다.|새 도구를 무조건 많이 산 것이다.|예약 기록을 없앤 것이다.|Shorter appointments allowed everyone to finish.|짧은 작업에 긴 시간을 잡아 두던 예약을 조정해 공동 이용이 가능해졌습니다.
suneung-2000|25|2|A Pause in the Lecture|A lecturer explained a complicated process without stopping. Her audience copied every word, but few could describe the main idea afterward. During the next lecture, she added short pauses and asked listeners to explain one step to a partner. These breaks gave them time to digest the information. The session contained fewer slides but produced a clearer understanding of the process.|두 번째 강의에서 쉼의 역할은 무엇이었나요?|들은 내용을 이해하고 정리할 시간을 주었다.|주요 개념을 모두 빼 버렸다.|학생들의 설명을 막았다.|첫 강의보다 슬라이드를 더 빨리 넘겼다.|These breaks gave them time to digest the information.|중간에 멈추고 설명하는 시간이 정보의 이해를 도왔습니다.
suneung-2000|26|1|Beyond the Royal Costume|A fiction club wrote a story about a royal family. Their first draft described magnificent clothing but gave the characters no difficult choices. A reader said the setting was fancy while the plot felt empty. The writers added a conflict over a scarce resource and showed how each character responded. The story improved because decisions revealed character more clearly than decoration alone could.|이야기가 나아진 이유는 무엇인가요?|어려운 선택을 통해 인물의 성격이 드러났기 때문이다.|의상 설명만 더 길어졌기 때문이다.|모든 갈등을 없앴기 때문이다.|배경 장식의 수만 늘렸기 때문이다.|decisions revealed character more clearly than decoration alone could|자원 부족에 대응하는 선택이 외형적 장식보다 인물을 잘 보여 줬습니다.
suneung-2000|26|2|A Client's Priorities|A generous client offered to finance a new garden bench. She initially chose an expensive design because she liked its fancy appearance. The park staff explained that frequent repairs would require money the park did not have. They compared durability and utility before choosing a simpler bench. The client's gift became more useful when it considered the future care of the object as well as its purchase.|더 단순한 벤치를 선택한 이유는?|구매 후 관리 비용과 실제 쓰임까지 고려했기 때문이다.|의뢰인이 기부를 완전히 취소했기 때문이다.|아무도 벤치를 사용할 수 없게 하려고 했다.|비싼 디자인이 항상 불법이기 때문이다.|They compared durability and utility before choosing a simpler bench.|겉모습뿐 아니라 내구성과 유용성, 이후 관리 가능성을 따졌습니다.
suneung-2000|27|1|Reading the Abstract|A contemporary science article began with an abstract that seemed to confirm a dramatic discovery. Our class read the full explanation before accepting the claim. The experiment involved only a small number of samples, and the authors said that more tests were necessary. The abstract had summarized a promising result, not settled every question. Careful reading helped us distinguish a possibility from a firm conclusion.|학생들이 발견한 연구의 한계는?|표본이 적고 추가 시험이 필요했다는 것이다.|논문에 실험이 전혀 없었다는 것이다.|초록이 모든 질문을 최종 해결했다는 것이다.|연구자들이 원문 읽기를 금지했다는 것이다.|The experiment involved only a small number of samples, and the authors said that more tests were necessary.|초록의 긍정적 요약만으로 확정된 결론이라고 볼 수 없었습니다.
suneung-2000|27|2|Measuring the Cottage|We wanted to apply a new coat of paint to a small cottage. A neighbor estimated the quantity by looking at the building from the road. Another offered to measure each wall and subtract the windows. The second method took longer but produced a more useful estimate. We bought enough paint without a large surplus, confirming the value of a little careful preparation.|두 번째 견적 방법의 장점은 무엇인가요?|벽과 창의 실제 크기를 반영했다.|길에서 본 느낌만 사용했다.|창문 면적까지 무조건 두 번 계산했다.|페인트 양을 무작위로 골랐다.|Another offered to measure each wall and subtract the windows.|벽 면적에서 칠하지 않을 창을 빼 더 적절한 양을 계산했습니다.
suneung-2000|28|1|The Scholarship Proposal|A pupil hoped to win a scholarship for a project about local stories. Her first document listed many impressive activities but did not explain their purpose. A teacher asked her to propose one clear question and connect each activity to it. The revised plan was smaller but easier to understand. It showed how interviews and drawings would work together, rather than merely promising a busy schedule.|계획을 고칠 때 가장 중요했던 변화는?|하나의 질문에 활동들을 연결한 것이다.|인상적인 활동 이름만 더 많이 적은 것이다.|목적 설명을 모두 없앤 것이다.|다른 사람과의 인터뷰를 무조건 금지한 것이다.|A teacher asked her to propose one clear question and connect each activity to it.|명확한 질문을 중심으로 활동의 목적과 관계를 드러냈습니다.
suneung-2000|28|2|Signs at the Resort|A resort planned to install signs that simply said which activities were prohibited. Visitors often ignored them because they did not understand the reason. New signs explained that a fragile nesting area lay beyond the fence and suggested another path. More guests followed the guidance. The change did not remove the rule; it made the rule's purpose visible and offered an appropriate alternative.|새 표지판이 더 효과적이었던 이유는?|금지 이유와 이용 가능한 다른 길을 설명했기 때문이다.|규칙을 완전히 없앴기 때문이다.|방문객에게 아무 정보도 주지 않았기 때문이다.|울타리 너머로 들어가라고 권했기 때문이다.|New signs explained that a fragile nesting area lay beyond the fence and suggested another path.|보호해야 할 이유와 대체 경로를 함께 알려 주었습니다.
suneung-2000|29|1|The Wrong Address|Our club ordered materials through a virtual shop. The receipt showed our old address, although we had entered the new one on the final page. Before abandoning the order, we contacted a supervisor with a copy of both records. The shop identified a defect in its saved address form. Correcting the information before shipping prevented a much longer delay later.|배송 지연을 예방할 수 있었던 행동은?|영수증의 주소를 확인하고 출고 전에 기록을 전달한 것이다.|오류를 보고도 아무 연락을 하지 않은 것이다.|주문을 받자마자 영수증을 모두 버린 것이다.|잘못된 주소로 배송될 때까지 기다린 것이다.|Correcting the information before shipping prevented a much longer delay later.|출고 전에 주소 오류를 발견하고 바로잡았습니다.
suneung-2000|29|2|Comparing the Earthquake Models|Students built two model towers for an earthquake demonstration. One group claimed that its design was stronger because the tower appeared wider. A supervisor asked them to compare both models under the same shaking pattern. The wider tower lost a loose upper section first. The class learned to inspect particular weaknesses rather than treating one visible trait as proof of overall strength.|실험이 보여 준 것은 무엇인가요?|눈에 보이는 한 특징만으로 전체 강도를 판단하면 안 된다.|폭이 넓은 탑은 언제나 가장 튼튼하다.|두 모형을 서로 다른 조건에서 시험해야 한다.|흔들림 없이 외관만 비교하면 충분하다.|The wider tower lost a loose upper section first.|넓어 보이는 탑도 느슨한 부분이 먼저 손상돼 세부 약점을 살펴야 했습니다.
suneung-2000|30|1|Nutrition on the Contract|A student market planned to sell snack boxes. The retail contract focused on price and packaging, but a team member asked about nutrition. They added criteria for clear ingredient information and portion size before choosing a supplier. The boxes remained affordable. The change showed that a purchasing decision could include useful information for customers instead of considering only the cheapest offer.|계약에 새로 반영한 기준은?|재료 정보와 한 번에 제공되는 분량이었다.|포장 색깔만 같게 하는 조건이었다.|가장 싼 제안만 무조건 고르는 조건이었다.|고객에게 음식 정보를 숨기는 조건이었다.|They added criteria for clear ingredient information and portion size before choosing a supplier.|가격과 포장에 더해 소비자에게 필요한 식품 정보를 기준에 넣었습니다.
suneung-2000|30|2|Nurturing the Small Garden|During a fictional town-planning exercise, students designed a garden for a place with harsh summers and frequent drought. Their first drawing contained delicate plants that required constant watering. After reviewing the conditions, they chose plants suited to the proposed environment and added shade. The revised garden was less colorful on paper, but the students could explain how they would nurture it with limited water.|수정한 정원의 장점은 무엇인가요?|주어진 환경과 물의 제약 속에서 관리 방법을 설명할 수 있었다.|물을 무한히 사용할 수 있게 됐다.|지역 조건과 무관하게 식물을 골랐다.|그림의 색깔 수만 최대로 늘렸다.|the students could explain how they would nurture it with limited water|환경과 물의 한계를 고려해 유지 가능한 설계로 바꿨습니다.
suneung-2000|31|1|A Temporary Quiet Hour|The student council introduced a temporary quiet hour in a shared study room. During that hour, visitors could read alone; afterward, ordinary conversation was welcome again. The routine helped students who were sensitive to noise without depriving everyone else of a place to talk. A notice explained both times clearly, so people could choose the part of the day that suited their needs.|이 규칙이 서로 다른 요구를 배려한 방법은?|시간을 나누어 조용한 공부와 대화를 모두 가능하게 했다.|하루 종일 모두의 대화를 금지했다.|소음에 민감한 학생의 출입을 막았다.|안내문에 시간을 적지 않았다.|During that hour, visitors could read alone; afterward, ordinary conversation was welcome again.|시간대를 구분해 개인 학습과 대화 공간의 필요를 함께 반영했습니다.
suneung-2000|31|2|The Arctic Notebook|In a fictional explorer's notebook, an adolescent assistant records an arctic journey. The leader describes only distances and supplies, while the assistant notices sounds, colors, and moments of fear. Neither account can capture the whole experience alone. Read together, they show how a single journey may contain both practical problems and personal responses. Different observers can preserve different parts of the same event.|두 기록을 함께 읽을 때 얻는 것은?|하나의 여정에 관한 서로 보완하는 관점이다.|두 사람이 서로 다른 곳에 갔다는 증거다.|보급 기록은 항상 거짓이라는 결론이다.|개인의 감정은 기록할 가치가 없다는 결론이다.|Neither account can capture the whole experience alone.|실무적 정보와 개인적 경험이 각각 다른 부분을 담아 함께 읽으면 더 넓게 이해할 수 있습니다.
suneung-2000|32|1|An Errand Across Town|On a small errand, I discovered that a new barrier blocked the usual pedestrian crossing. A neighbor showed me a safer route through a public square. It took a few minutes longer, but I could cross at marked signals throughout the walk. I wrote the route down for my younger brother, including landmarks rather than relying on a vague instruction to take the next road.|화자가 동생을 위해 기록한 길 안내의 특징은?|눈에 보이는 표식을 포함해 구체적으로 설명했다.|다음 길로 가라는 말만 남겼다.|보행자 신호가 없는 곳만 골랐다.|막힌 횡단보도를 그대로 건너라고 했다.|I wrote the route down for my younger brother, including landmarks rather than relying on a vague instruction to take the next road.|모호한 방향 대신 표식을 넣어 동생이 안전한 길을 찾기 쉽게 했습니다.
suneung-2000|32|2|A Tradition in Two Voices|For a class play about an imaginary tribe, two characters discuss a tradition. One remembers the comfort it offered during a difficult childhood. The other describes an errand that the tradition makes harder today. Their statements are different without being dishonest. The audience must distinguish a person's experience from a complete judgment of the custom, and consider why the same practice can carry different meanings.|두 인물의 말에 대한 설명으로 알맞은 것은?|서로 다른 경험에 근거하므로 둘 다 진실할 수 있다.|한쪽이 다르면 반드시 거짓말이다.|과거의 경험만 현재에도 유효하다.|관습은 모든 사람에게 항상 같은 의미다.|Their statements are different without being dishonest.|같은 관습을 다른 경험과 필요에서 바라보므로 상반된 진술이 함께 성립할 수 있습니다.
suneung-2000|33|1|Protecting a Shared Project|A gardening team wanted to protect its progress during a week of hot weather. One peer suggested pouring a large amount of water onto every bed at once. Another checked the soil in each section and found that some areas were already wet. The team adjusted the plan to match the different beds. A drastic action across the whole garden was less useful than a careful response to local conditions.|팀이 화단별로 계획을 조정한 이유는?|흙의 상태가 위치마다 달랐기 때문이다.|모든 화단이 똑같이 말라 있었기 때문이다.|물의 양을 확인할 수 없었기 때문이다.|화단을 전부 없애기로 했기 때문이다.|Another checked the soil in each section and found that some areas were already wet.|이미 젖어 있는 구역도 있어 같은 양을 일괄 적용하지 않았습니다.
suneung-2000|33|2|The Germ Illustration|An illustrated story showed a germ as a tiny character carrying a weapon. Younger readers enjoyed the picture, but some thought real germs looked like the character. The artist added a short note separating the imaginary design from a simplified scientific diagram. The story could remain entertaining without presenting its visual invention as a literal description of infection or the things that cause it.|작가가 설명을 추가한 목적은?|상상 속 표현과 실제 설명을 구별하게 하려는 것이었다.|모든 그림이 실제 모습이라고 주장하려는 것이었다.|독자가 이야기를 읽지 못하게 하려는 것이었다.|과학 도표와 캐릭터를 완전히 같은 것으로 만들려는 것이었다.|The artist added a short note separating the imaginary design from a simplified scientific diagram.|재미를 위한 의인화 그림을 실제 과학적 모습으로 오해하지 않도록 구분했습니다.
suneung-2000|34|1|An Oral History of the Crop|A community project collected oral accounts of a crop once grown near the town. Older residents described planting times, family meals, and changes in the surrounding ecosystem. Their memories were valuable, but the interviewers also checked dates in written records. The final display placed recollections beside those records, allowing visitors to see both the personal meaning of the crop and the evidence for specific historical claims.|전시가 구술 기억과 문서를 나란히 둔 이유는?|개인적 의미와 역사적 근거를 함께 보여 주기 위해서였다.|모든 기억을 사실이 아니라고 버리기 위해서였다.|문서 기록을 읽지 못하게 하려는 것이었다.|작물의 이름을 숨기기 위해서였다.|the interviewers also checked dates in written records|기억의 가치를 살리면서 구체적인 날짜와 역사적 주장은 별도 기록으로 확인했습니다.
suneung-2000|34|2|The Candidate's Promise|In a mock election, a candidate promised to repair every broken appliance in the school at no cost. Another representative asked who would supply replacement parts. The first speaker admitted that the promise had ignored a real expense. Her revised proposal prioritized urgent repairs and explained the budget. She gained less applause during the speech but more trust during the later discussion.|수정한 공약이 신뢰를 얻은 이유는?|실제 비용과 우선순위를 설명했기 때문이다.|비용이 전혀 없다는 주장을 더 크게 했기 때문이다.|질문을 한 대표를 토론에서 제외했기 때문이다.|고장 난 기구를 모두 숨겼기 때문이다.|Her revised proposal prioritized urgent repairs and explained the budget.|현실적인 비용과 실행 순서를 제시해 검토 가능한 제안이 됐습니다.
suneung-2000|35|1|The Solar Comparison|For a solar energy exhibit, a team compared two model roofs under a lamp. The entire display suggested that one design always produced the best result. A visitor asked whether the angle of the light had stayed the same. It had not. The team repeated the comparison under matching conditions and revised its initial claim. A valid comparison required controlling the test, not just recording an impressive number.|첫 비교에서 확인이 필요했던 조건은?|빛이 들어오는 각도가 같았는지 여부였다.|지붕 모형의 이름이 같았는지 여부였다.|방문객이 몇 명 박수쳤는지 여부였다.|가장 큰 숫자를 지울 수 있는지 여부였다.|A visitor asked whether the angle of the light had stayed the same.|두 모형에 비추는 각도가 달라 공정한 비교인지 다시 확인해야 했습니다.
suneung-2000|35|2|Beyond the Hero's Beard|A student drew a hero with a long beard and an outstanding costume. In the accompanying story, however, the hero escaped every hardship by chance. The class found the pictures memorable but the character difficult to understand. The writer added a scene in which the hero confronted a problem and accepted a cost to help someone else. That choice gave the character more depth than another decorative detail would.|영웅에게 깊이를 더해 준 것은 무엇인가요?|타인을 위해 대가를 감수하는 선택이었다.|수염 길이를 더 늘린 것이었다.|모든 어려움을 우연히 피하게 한 것이었다.|옷의 장식만 추가한 것이었다.|The writer added a scene in which the hero confronted a problem and accepted a cost to help someone else|외모가 아니라 어려운 상황에서 내린 선택이 인물을 이해할 단서를 주었습니다.
suneung-2000|36|1|Threads of a Dynasty|For an imaginary dynasty in a school play, the costume team gave each family a different thread color. The colors quietly connected characters across several scenes. The design used minimal decoration, yet it helped the audience follow relationships that the dialogue did not always explain. A final review checked that the pattern remained consistent. One small visual choice could unify a complicated story.|실 색깔이 한 역할은 무엇인가요?|인물 사이의 가족 관계를 시각적으로 연결했다.|대사의 모든 내용을 대신 읽어 주었다.|장면마다 관계를 무작위로 바꿨다.|모든 인물을 같은 사람으로 보이게 했다.|The colors quietly connected characters across several scenes.|일관된 색깔이 대사로 설명하지 않은 관계를 따라갈 수 있게 했습니다.
suneung-2000|36|2|The Raft Workshop|At a craft workshop, we built a small raft from reused wood. One board began to soak up water, causing that side to sit lower. Instead of adding decoration to hide the tilt, we stopped and reviewed the construction. Replacing the damaged board restored balance. The workshop reminded us that improving a visible surface cannot repair a weakness in the part supporting it.|기울어진 뗏목을 바로잡은 조치는?|물이 스며드는 손상된 판자를 교체한 것이다.|기울어진 쪽에 장식만 붙인 것이다.|문제를 보지 않으려고 색을 칠한 것이다.|모든 점검을 중단한 것이다.|Replacing the damaged board restored balance.|겉모습이 아니라 균형을 무너뜨린 구조적 원인을 고쳤습니다.
suneung-2000|37|1|A Session about Ingredients|During a budgeting session, students planned a meal for a fictional camping trip. One expensive ingredient used nearly half their money, although it appeared in only a small garnish. They chose a simpler garnish and preserved the main dishes. The revised budget still matched their planned menu. They had not removed every pleasure; they had distinguished an optional detail from the parts needed to feed the group.|학생들이 바꾼 것은 무엇인가요?|비싼 장식을 줄이고 주요 요리는 유지한 것이다.|식사 계획 전체를 취소한 것이다.|주요 요리를 모두 장식으로 바꾼 것이다.|예산을 확인하지 않고 재료를 산 것이다.|They chose a simpler garnish and preserved the main dishes.|핵심 식사와 선택적인 장식을 구분해 예산을 조정했습니다.
suneung-2000|37|2|The Fragile Antique|A museum guide presented an antique bowl with a visible crack. A visitor asked why the staff had not made it look completely new. The guide explained that the crack formed part of the object's history, while careful support kept it safe for display. The goal was not to erase every sign of age. It was to preserve a fragile object without pretending that time had never touched it.|박물관이 추구한 보존 목표는?|시간의 흔적을 감추지 않으면서 물건을 안전하게 유지하는 것이었다.|모든 옛 흔적을 없애 새 제품처럼 만드는 것이었다.|깨진 그릇을 관람객이 직접 만지게 하는 것이었다.|그릇의 실제 역사를 숨기는 것이었다.|The goal was not to erase every sign of age.|보존은 모든 노화 흔적의 삭제가 아니라 안전한 유지와 역사 전달을 함께 목표로 했습니다.
suneung-2000|38|1|The Circular Market|Students designed an imaginary market around a circular square. They placed every popular stall at the same entrance, creating congestion in their model. Moving a few stalls to other sides improved the flow without changing the square itself. The obstacle had been the arrangement of activity, not the shape alone. The class learned to test how people would move through a design before praising its appearance.|혼잡의 주된 원인은 무엇이었나요?|인기 있는 가판대가 한 입구에 몰려 있었기 때문이다.|광장이 원형이면 이동이 절대 불가능하기 때문이다.|가판대가 하나도 없었기 때문이다.|사람들이 어느 곳에도 멈추지 않았기 때문이다.|They placed every popular stall at the same entrance, creating congestion in their model.|모양 자체보다 활동이 특정 입구에 집중된 배치가 문제였습니다.
suneung-2000|38|2|An Ancestor's Rough Map|An ancestor's notebook contained a rough map of fields and a short account of barter. Our family could identify the river, but several other marks were vague. Rather than invent names for them, we compared the map with later records and labeled uncertain points as questions. The investigation remained unfinished, yet it gave us a clearer distinction between what we knew and what we merely suspected.|가족이 모호한 표시를 다룬 방법은?|기록을 비교하고 불확실한 부분은 질문으로 남겼다.|근거 없이 장소 이름을 만들어 적었다.|모든 표시가 이미 정확하다고 선언했다.|강이 그려져 있다는 사실까지 지웠다.|we compared the map with later records and labeled uncertain points as questions|확인된 사실과 아직 모르는 내용을 구분했습니다.
suneung-2000|39|1|The Opponent's Thanks|After losing a debate, my opponent thanked the volunteers who had organized the room. I had expected a miserable expression or an angry complaint. Instead, she explained that disappointment about a result did not remove her gratitude for other people's work. Her response changed the way I viewed competition. We could oppose an argument strongly without treating everyone involved as an enemy.|상대의 행동이 보여 준 태도는?|결과에 실망해도 다른 사람의 노력에는 감사할 수 있다.|패배하면 모든 관계를 끊어야 한다.|토론에서는 감사 표현이 금지된다.|상대 의견에 반대하면 반드시 사람도 미워해야 한다.|disappointment about a result did not remove her gratitude for other people's work|결과에 대한 감정과 타인의 수고에 대한 감사를 구분했습니다.
suneung-2000|39|2|Portraying the Fossil|A student artist wanted to portray a fossil animal for an exhibit. The fossil showed some bones clearly, while other parts were missing. She used different shades to separate observed shapes from imagined details. Visitors could enjoy the full picture and still see where evidence ended. The illustration was more informative because it made uncertainty visible rather than hiding it under a confident appearance.|그림에서 음영을 다르게 한 목적은?|관찰된 부분과 상상으로 채운 부분을 구분하려는 것이었다.|모든 부분이 화석에 남아 있다고 주장하려는 것이었다.|그림 전체를 보이지 않게 하려는 것이었다.|발견된 뼈를 없는 것으로 표시하려는 것이었다.|She used different shades to separate observed shapes from imagined details.|확인된 증거와 재구성한 부분을 시각적으로 구분했습니다.
suneung-2000|40|1|Layers in the Pottery|At a pottery workshop, a visitor admired the radiant surface of a finished bowl. The maker showed an earlier piece with uneven layers beneath its polish. Those layers had caused the surface to crack. She explained that the attractive finish depended on careful work that was no longer visible. The visitor began to value the hidden stages of making the bowl as well as its final appearance.|완성된 그릇의 겉모습이 의존한 것은?|보이지 않게 된 아래 단계의 정성스러운 작업이었다.|광택만 여러 번 덧씌우는 일이었다.|고르지 않은 층을 그대로 두는 일이었다.|이전 제작 과정을 모두 무시하는 일이었다.|the attractive finish depended on careful work that was no longer visible|최종 표면의 품질은 그 아래 층을 잘 만드는 과정에서 시작됐습니다.
suneung-2000|40|2|Choosing a Property|Our art class compared two apples for a drawing. One variety had a stronger flavor, but nobody would eat the fruit during this lesson. That property was irrelevant to the task. We chose the apple with clearer patches of light and shadow instead. The exercise helped us justify a choice using a quality connected to its purpose, rather than a detail that mattered only in another situation.|그림을 그릴 사과를 고를 때 중요하게 본 것은?|그림에 필요한 빛과 그림자의 모습이었다.|먹었을 때의 맛만이었다.|가장 재미있는 이름이었다.|과제 목적과 관계없는 이야기였다.|We chose the apple with clearer patches of light and shadow instead.|먹는 맛이 아니라 그리는 활동의 목적에 맞는 시각적 특성을 선택했습니다.
suneung-2000|41|1|Welcoming the Colleague|At a conference reception, a new colleague stood beside the door while everyone discussed shared memories. Nobody intended to exclude her, but the conversation gave her little chance to join. One organizer asked about her current project and connected it with another guest's work. The small change enhanced the reception more than an elaborate decoration would have. Welcome depended on interaction, not merely on sending an invitation.|새 동료가 대화에 참여하게 된 계기는?|현재 하는 일을 묻고 다른 참석자의 작업과 연결한 것이었다.|기존 추억만 더 자세히 이야기한 것이었다.|출입문 옆에서 계속 기다리게 한 것이었다.|초대장을 회수한 것이었다.|One organizer asked about her current project and connected it with another guest's work.|새 참석자가 자신의 경험으로 기여할 수 있는 대화 주제를 마련했습니다.
suneung-2000|41|2|The Copper Object|A small copper object lay among things that a workshop planned to discard. Its surface had darkened, so it seemed to have little merit. An older member recognized it as a tool used by the workshop's founder. They cleaned it carefully and placed it beside a written account of that work. Its importance came from the history people cherished, not from looking new or costly.|그 물건의 가치가 새롭게 드러난 이유는?|작업장의 역사와 연결된 도구였기 때문이다.|새것처럼 반짝이는 표면을 가졌기 때문이다.|가장 비싼 금속으로 만들어졌기 때문이다.|아무도 어떤 물건인지 몰랐기 때문이다.|An older member recognized it as a tool used by the workshop's founder.|창립자가 사용한 도구라는 맥락이 외관만으로 보이지 않던 가치를 알려 줬습니다.
suneung-2000|42|1|Testing the Bond|Two groups used different materials to bond pieces of a cardboard model. The first group called its result superior because the joint looked neat. The second suggested a common procedure: both models should hold the same small weight for the same length of time. Testing with caution revealed that the neater joint loosened first. Appearance had provided a clue, but not a complete measure of strength.|두 모형을 비교한 공정한 절차는?|같은 무게를 같은 시간 동안 지지하게 하는 것이었다.|보기 좋은 모형에만 더 가벼운 무게를 주는 것이었다.|접합부를 보지 않고 승자를 정하는 것이었다.|한 모형만 시험하고 다른 것은 제외하는 것이었다.|both models should hold the same small weight for the same length of time|하중과 시간을 같게 해 접합 강도를 비교했습니다.
suneung-2000|42|2|A Useful Caution|An art teacher gave a simple procedure for cutting paper shapes. The demonstration looked easy, so some students hurried to copy it. She paused to explain why each caution mattered and showed where hands should remain away from the moving tool. Students then practiced slowly under supervision. Understanding the reason for a precaution helped them follow the procedure more carefully than memorizing a warning alone.|교사가 주의 사항의 이유까지 설명한 목적은?|절차를 이해하고 더 신중하게 따르게 하려는 것이었다.|빠르게 따라 하는 경쟁을 시키려는 것이었다.|경고를 외울 필요가 없으니 모두 지우려는 것이었다.|도구를 아무 감독 없이 쓰게 하려는 것이었다.|Understanding the reason for a precaution helped them follow the procedure more carefully than memorizing a warning alone.|주의의 이유를 이해하는 것이 말만 외우는 것보다 신중한 행동을 도왔습니다.
suneung-2000|43|1|Architecture beside the Wilderness|A design class imagined a visitor center beside a wilderness area. One model used a tall tower to attract attention; another kept the architecture low and directed people toward existing paths. The class compared the likely outcome of each plan for both visitors and the surrounding landscape. The quieter design won because the goal was to help people experience the place, not to make the building dominate it.|낮은 건물 설계가 선택된 이유는?|건물이 주변 장소의 경험을 돕는 것이 목적이었기 때문이다.|방문객이 자연을 전혀 보지 못하게 하려는 것이었다.|가장 높은 건물이 언제나 안전하지 않기 때문이다.|모든 길을 새로 만들기 위해서였다.|the goal was to help people experience the place, not to make the building dominate it|주의를 독점하는 건물보다 주변 장소를 경험하게 하는 설계를 우선했습니다.
suneung-2000|43|2|The Compact List|For a field visit, our group prepared a compact equipment list. We almost omitted a map because everyone carried a phone. A partner asked what would happen if a battery failed. We added a printed map to the shared bag rather than asking every person to bring one. The strategy kept the list short while preserving a useful backup. Compact did not have to mean unprepared.|짐을 간단하게 하면서 대비한 방법은?|여럿이 함께 쓸 종이 지도 한 장을 넣은 것이다.|휴대전화가 절대 고장 나지 않는다고 가정한 것이다.|모든 지도를 목록에서 삭제한 것이다.|한 사람마다 큰 장비를 똑같이 준비한 것이다.|We added a printed map to the shared bag rather than asking every person to bring one.|공용 예비 지도를 마련해 짐의 양과 대비 필요를 함께 고려했습니다.
suneung-2000|44|1|Restoring the Vessel|A craft student tried to restore a broken clay vessel. She wanted the joint to disappear completely, but the instructor asked what the object would be used for. It was a teaching sample meant to show a repair technique. A visible, carefully supported joint served that purpose better. The student learned that an adequate restoration depends partly on the object's intended role, not only on hiding damage.|접합부를 보이게 둔 이유는?|수리 기법을 가르치는 표본의 목적에 맞았기 때문이다.|수리를 전혀 할 수 없었기 때문이다.|모든 그릇은 손상을 숨기면 안 되기 때문이다.|그릇의 사용 목적을 알 수 없었기 때문이다.|It was a teaching sample meant to show a repair technique.|교육용 표본이므로 잘 수리된 접합 부분을 볼 수 있는 것이 도움이 됐습니다.
suneung-2000|44|2|An Optimistic Plan|We planned a paper decoration for a school event, confident that it would last all afternoon. The hall became humid, however, and the delicate pieces began to bend. An optimistic organizer suggested moving them away from the damp entrance and replacing only the damaged parts. We did not need to restore every piece to its original position. A flexible response kept the event welcoming despite the changed conditions.|행사를 유지하기 위해 취한 대응은?|습한 입구를 피해 옮기고 손상된 부분만 교체했다.|모든 장식을 반드시 처음 자리에 돌려놓았다.|행사를 즉시 영구 중단했다.|휘어진 장식을 관람객에게 숨기기만 했다.|An optimistic organizer suggested moving them away from the damp entrance and replacing only the damaged parts.|조건의 변화에 맞춰 위치와 필요한 부분만 조정했습니다.
suneung-2000|45|1|A Transaction with Information|At a pretend school market, teams traded handmade goods using paper tokens. One group recorded only the number of tokens in each transaction. Another also recorded which product had been exchanged and whether a customer requested a change. At the end, the second group could explain its customers' concerns more clearly. Counting sales showed activity; recording the context helped the students understand that activity.|두 번째 모둠의 기록에서 얻을 수 있었던 것은?|어떤 제품과 요청이 거래에 관련됐는지에 대한 이해였다.|토큰 수와 전혀 무관한 소문뿐이었다.|첫 모둠보다 항상 더 많은 판매량이었다.|고객 의견을 모두 무시할 근거였다.|the second group could explain its customers' concerns more clearly|물품과 요청을 함께 적어 숫자 뒤의 맥락을 설명할 수 있었습니다.
suneung-2000|45|2|Listening to the Concern|A classmate expressed anxiety about presenting a project. I offered casual advice to stop worrying, but he grew quieter. Another student asked which part concerned him most. He knew the material but was afraid the tablet would fail during his demonstration. We prepared printed diagrams as a backup. Sympathy became more useful when it included listening to the specific concern instead of assuming that every worry had the same cause.|구체적인 도움을 줄 수 있었던 이유는?|발표 자료를 보여 줄 기기의 고장을 걱정한다는 점을 알았기 때문이다.|내용을 전혀 몰랐다고 단정했기 때문이다.|걱정을 무시하고 발표를 강요했기 때문이다.|모든 불안의 원인이 같다고 정했기 때문이다.|He knew the material but was afraid the tablet would fail during his demonstration.|걱정의 실제 원인을 확인해 출력 자료라는 대비책을 마련했습니다.
suneung-2000|46|1|The Tenant's Proposal|A tenant noticed that a broken section of pavement made the route to the municipal garden awkward. She could have sent a vague complaint about the neighborhood. Instead, she marked the location on a map and described who had difficulty passing it. Her specific report helped workers inspect the right place quickly. A worthwhile proposal connected a general concern with details that someone could act on.|보고가 바로 점검으로 이어질 수 있었던 이유는?|위치와 불편을 겪는 사람을 구체적으로 설명했기 때문이다.|동네 전체가 나쁘다는 말만 했기 때문이다.|어디인지 알 수 없는 사진만 보냈기 때문이다.|다른 사람의 이동과 관련이 없다고 주장했기 때문이다.|she marked the location on a map and described who had difficulty passing it|행동으로 옮길 수 있는 위치와 영향을 정확히 전달했습니다.
suneung-2000|46|2|The Symbolic Door|In a story set in an imaginary village, a sacred doorway stands open at every festival. A visitor assumes that the custom means anyone may enter the building at any time. A resident explains that the open door is symbolic, representing a welcome during the ceremony. The visitor learns to ask about a practice's local meaning rather than treating its appearance as a universal instruction.|방문객이 처음 오해한 것은 무엇인가요?|의식의 상징을 언제나 적용되는 출입 허가로 받아들였다.|문이 실제로 닫혀 있다고 생각했다.|마을에 축제가 전혀 없다고 생각했다.|주민이 상징의 뜻을 설명할 수 없다고 생각했다.|the open door is symbolic, representing a welcome during the ceremony|열린 문은 의식 중 환영을 나타내는 상징이지 모든 시간의 출입 허가는 아니었습니다.
suneung-2000|47|1|Revising the Proposal|A class judge had to choose among several designs for a reading corner. Rather than award the prize immediately, she gave each team a prompt describing one practical concern. The teams could revise their proposals before the final decision. This process allowed diverse ideas to improve without forcing them into an identical design. The judge's role included supporting better thinking, not merely ranking the first attempts.|수정 기회를 준 목적은 무엇인가요?|서로 다른 설계가 실용적 문제를 검토하며 발전하게 하려는 것이었다.|모든 설계를 완전히 똑같게 만들려는 것이었다.|처음 제출한 것을 수정 없이 순위만 매기려는 것이었다.|학생들의 생각을 대신 작성해 주려는 것이었다.|This process allowed diverse ideas to improve without forcing them into an identical design.|한 가지 모양으로 통일하지 않고 각 아이디어의 개선을 도왔습니다.
suneung-2000|47|2|An Iceberg Exhibit|An exhibit about an imaginary polar expedition included a large iceberg model and several mammal figures. Flashing lights attracted visitors, but the movement distracted them from the explanation of the animals' routes. The designer reduced the flashing and added a simple moving line. Visitors could follow the intended information more easily. A dynamic display was useful only when its activity supported the idea it was meant to explain.|전시의 움직임을 조정한 이유는?|시각 효과가 핵심 설명을 돕도록 하기 위해서였다.|모든 정보와 그림을 없애기 위해서였다.|관람객이 동물의 경로를 보지 못하게 하려고 했다.|깜박이는 불빛을 더 복잡하게 늘리려고 했다.|Visitors could follow the intended information more easily.|주의를 빼앗던 효과를 줄이고 설명에 맞는 움직임을 남겼습니다.
suneung-2000|48|1|A Deliberate Pause|In a short play, a character gives a monologue about a priceless gift. The actor originally rushed through the final line, leaving the audience unsure why the gift mattered. A deliberate pause before that line changed the scene. It gave listeners time to connect the object with an earlier loss in the plot. No new words were added, but the relationship between existing moments became clearer.|마지막 문장 앞의 침묵이 한 역할은?|관객이 선물과 이전의 상실을 연결할 시간을 주었다.|대사를 새로 추가했다.|이야기의 이전 사건을 완전히 없앴다.|선물이 아무 의미 없다고 단정했다.|It gave listeners time to connect the object with an earlier loss in the plot.|이미 제시된 사건 사이의 관계를 생각할 시간을 주었습니다.
suneung-2000|48|2|A Partial Repair|A model tower began to collapse during a practice test. The team replaced one broken rod and immediately prepared to celebrate. Their partner asked whether the neighboring joints had also been damaged. A second inspection found another loose connection. The partial repair had solved the visible problem but not every weakness. Their mutual goal was a reliable model, so checking again was part of completing the work.|추가 점검이 필요했던 이유는?|눈에 보이는 파손 외에도 다른 연결부가 느슨했기 때문이다.|처음 부러진 막대가 실제로는 멀쩡했기 때문이다.|모형을 고칠 목적이 전혀 없었기 때문이다.|성공을 축하하면 점검이 불가능하기 때문이다.|A second inspection found another loose connection.|한 부분의 교체만으로 인접한 손상까지 모두 해결된 것은 아니었습니다.
suneung-2000|49|1|Reading the Brochure|A course brochure promised an exciting curriculum, but it said little about how learners would demonstrate progress. Before joining, our study group asked for an example assessment and the expected weekly work. The answers gave the bright slogans a practical context. We could then decide whether the course matched our needs. An attractive description became useful when supported by details about what participation actually involved.|추가 질문이 도움이 된 이유는?|실제 과제와 평가를 알아 과정이 필요에 맞는지 판단할 수 있었다.|홍보 문구의 색깔만 비교할 수 있었다.|과정을 완수하지 않아도 자동 수료된다고 알게 됐다.|모든 과정이 똑같다는 증거를 얻었다.|We could then decide whether the course matched our needs.|학습 활동과 평가의 구체적인 정보가 선택의 근거가 됐습니다.
suneung-2000|49|2|The Model Vehicle Race|Our group entered a model vehicle race and hoped to gain speed by adding a stronger push. A teammate noticed that one wheel rubbed against the deck. Fixing that contact improved the vehicle before we changed the starting force. The result showed why an assessment should examine obstacles as well as possible additions. More input does not automatically help when part of the system is wasting it.|속도를 높이기 전에 먼저 해결한 것은?|바퀴가 차체 부분에 마찰하던 문제였다.|출발하는 힘이 강한 문제였다.|경주 규칙에 바퀴가 없는 문제였다.|밀어 주는 사람의 수가 너무 많은 문제였다.|Fixing that contact improved the vehicle before we changed the starting force.|추진력을 더하기 전에 에너지가 낭비되는 마찰을 줄였습니다.
suneung-2000|50|1|A Bystander's Choice|During a rehearsal, one student began to tease another about a small mistake. A bystander noticed that the laughter had become uncomfortable. She interrupted calmly, suggested practicing the difficult part together, and asked the teacher for help when the teasing continued. The rehearsal could remain lively without becoming aggressive. Supporting a classmate did not require turning the situation into another contest of insults.|방관하지 않은 학생이 한 행동은?|함께 연습하자고 제안하고 계속되는 놀림에 교사의 도움을 구했다.|더 강한 모욕으로 맞섰다.|불편해하는 학생에게 웃으라고 강요했다.|놀림이 재미있다며 계속 응원했다.|She interrupted calmly, suggested practicing the difficult part together, and asked the teacher for help when the teasing continued.|상대를 공격하기보다 연습 방향으로 돌리고 필요한 도움을 요청했습니다.
suneung-2000|50|2|The Revised Blueprint|A workshop team compared its blueprint with the first output of a simple model-making process. The rear section was too narrow because a copied measurement had lost a digit. They corrected the drawing and checked the next model before making several more. The breakdown was useful information about the process, not proof that every part of the plan was worthless. Finding one specific cause made a focused repair possible.|팀이 문제를 해결한 방식은?|잘못 옮긴 치수를 바로잡고 다음 모형으로 확인했다.|계획 전체가 무가치하다고 버렸다.|검사하지 않고 같은 모형을 계속 만들었다.|좁은 뒷부분을 설계와 관계없다고 무시했다.|They corrected the drawing and checked the next model before making several more.|원인이 된 치수를 수정하고 확인한 뒤 제작을 이어 갔습니다.
